//? Title: Learning-Rate Schedules: Clock, Endpoints and State
//? Subtitle: Evaluate the schedule at the completed-update counter before taking the next step
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A learning rate can vary during training, but the intended schedule depends on its clock
and endpoint conventions. We compare step, exponential, cosine, warmup, one-cycle and
cyclical native schedules at explicit update indices. A scalar quadratic has an exact
product solution, so it checks that scheduled SGD uses the correct rate before each
update. A zero-rate momentum example shows that suppressing parameter displacement does
not necessarily suppress optimizer-state changes. No dataset or universal rate-selection
claim is needed for these controlled checks.
)
\toc

> The method


The native schedule functions are pure functions of a nonnegative integer step. In these
loops step t is the number of completed optimizer calls, read from iterations() before
setting the rate for call t+1. It is not an epoch index unless the application deliberately
uses epochs as its clock. Always say which clock a plotted schedule uses.

Step and continuous exponential decay differ at the interval boundaries:

$$
 \eta_{step}(t)=\eta_0 c^{\lfloor t/I\rfloor},\qquad
 \eta_{exp}(t)=\eta_0 c^{t/I}.
$$
\alttext(Step decay uses an integer interval count; exponential decay uses a continuous exponent.)

Cosine interpolation from initial rate to a final rate clamps at its duration D:

$$
 \eta(t)=\eta_f+\frac{\eta_0-\eta_f}{2}
 [1+\cos(\pi\min(t,D)/D)].
$$
\alttext(Cosine interpolation reaches the final rate at the duration and keeps it thereafter.)

Warmup rises linearly from zero to its peak; a zero warmup duration disables the ramp.
Warmup-cosine joins the ramp to a cosine tail at the warmup boundary. One-cycle here
uses a cosine rise and fall of the learning rate only: momentum is not cycled.
Cyclical uses triangular cycles with an optional multiplicative amplitude reduction per
full cycle. Negative steps, nonfinite/negative rates and invalid shape parameters are
rejected. A valid schedule does not prove stability on a particular objective.


The concept diagram is generated from the retained local TikZ source. LaTeX with
PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm, is needed only to regenerate it.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/learning_rate_schedules_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/learning_rate_schedules_concept.tikz > build/learning_rate_schedules.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/learning_rate_schedules_concept.svg build/learning_rate_schedules_concept.dvi > build/learning_rate_schedules.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/learning_rate_schedules_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
