\caption(A direct product of scalar factors verifies the tensor trajectory and the before-update schedule convention.)
\alttext(Three scalar-parameter trajectories use constant, warmup-cosine and exponential schedules. The table compares final weights with the exact product reference and maximum numerical error.)

>> Zero rate is different from omitting an update

For momentum .9 and constant gradient one, use rates (.1,0,.1). The second
call changes no weight, but updates velocity to 1.9 and advances the counter. The
third velocity is 2.71. Omitting the second call instead yields velocity 1.9 on the
next call, so the final parameters differ. A schedule that temporarily reaches zero
must specify whether optimizer calls continue; these native calls do continue.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/schedule_zero_rate.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::nn::OptimizerConfig c;
  c.kind          = dm::nn::OptimizerKind::Momentum;
  const auto full = dm::nn::optimizer_gradient_trace(
                 {0}, dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>(3, 1, {1, 1, 1}), {.1, 0, .1}, c),
             skip = dm::nn::optimizer_gradient_trace(
                 {0}, dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>(2, 1, {1, 1}), {.1, .1}, c);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(
       {0, 1},
       {full.double_at("after", 2), skip.double_at("after", 1)},
       "final weight",
       {37, 99, 235},
       .5)
      .title("A zero-rate call still changes velocity")
      .x_label("0 = keep zero-rate call, 1 = omit call")
      .y_label("final parameter")
      .y_limits(-.6, .1);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(900, 430).save_svg("build/schedule_zero_rate.svg");
  std::print("Keep zero-rate call:\n\n{}\n\nOmit call:\n\n{}\n", full.to_md(6), skip.to_md(6));
}
```
