// result_end
\caption(Schedules control displacement rates; update counters and velocity can still change at rate zero.)
\alttext(Two bars compare final weights with a retained zero-rate call versus an omitted call. Tables expose rates, velocity and before/after parameters.)

> Conclusion

A schedule is a rate function plus a clock and an application convention. Endpoint
tables and an exactly soluble quadratic catch indexing errors; zero-rate calls expose
state changes hidden by a flat parameter trace. Schedule shapes alone do not establish
stability or generalization on another objective.

> References

- Loshchilov, I. and Hutter, F. (2017). [SGDR: Stochastic Gradient Descent with Warm Restarts|https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.03983]. Cosine annealing context; the native cosine function here is a single clamped segment, not a restart policy.
- Smith, L. N. (2017). [Cyclical Learning Rates for Training Neural Networks|https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01186]. Cyclical rate motivation; native one-cycle here changes the rate only.
